.TH DMESG 1
.SH NAME
dmesg \- print the kernel message ring
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmesg
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dmesg
prints the contents of the kernel's message ring, oldest character first.
.PP
Every character of every diagnostic the kernel prints goes to the operator's console
and, on the way, into a circular buffer of
.B MSGBUFS
characters
.RI ( putchar ()
in
.BR kernel/dev/sc.c ).
The buffer wraps and keeps no mark of where it did, so what
.I dmesg
prints is the last
.B MSGBUFS
characters the kernel produced, which normally begins part way through a line.
Carriage returns are not stored and neither are nulls, so a null in the buffer is a
character position the kernel has never yet reached; those are skipped.
.PP
.I Dmesg
takes no arguments and needs no privilege.
It reads the buffer and the cursor with two
.IR kctl (2)
calls and opens no device.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kctl(2), ps(1), pstat(1M)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Dmesg
complains if this kernel exports no
.B msgbuf
or
.BR msgbufp ,
or if the cursor does not point inside the buffer, and exits 1.
The last of those means the table in
.B kernel/ksym.c
is wrong; it cannot mean what v7's "Namelist mismatch" meant, since there is no
.B /unix
on this system for a namelist to be stale against.
.SH BUGS
.PP
.B Note:
.B this page is new.
.I Dmesg
is one of the two commands in the v7
.B /usr/src/cmd
tree that came with no manual page anywhere in the system; the other is yes.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.I dmesg
kept a copy of the buffer in
.B /usr/adm/msgbuf
and printed only what had appeared since the last run, with a date line above it and
.B ...
where the two copies diverged; its
.B \-
flag suppressed the update of that file.
Neither the file nor the directory is on this system and all of it is gone,
the date line included.
This
.I dmesg
prints the whole ring every time.
